Incumbent Jeff Hurd (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Jeff Hurd seeks a second term in a western Colorado district that leans Republican by about five points. He survived a primary challenge in June and enters the fall with a large financial edge, roughly $1.8 million on hand to about $84,000 for Democrat Dwayne Romero, who won a contested primary of his own. Ten expert ratings split among Likely Republican, Lean Republican and Toss-up, landing at a consensus Lean Republican.\n\nThe race has been active: the candidates have debated, and coverage highlights Hurd stressing his independence from his party. A July survey of likely voters from a Democratic-affiliated firm showed a one-point contest, the only fresh poll available. Betting markets put Democratic win chances in the mid-to-high 30s in percent terms. The evidence points to a Hurd lead of roughly three to four points, with genuine room for movement if the national climate shifts sharply.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2665, "margin": -3.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2864, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.288, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3508, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2424, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2636, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3101, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.3157, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3103, "margin": -3.5}]}